WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.233
Secure AI agent design uses layered controls.

2
00:00:03.233 --> 00:00:06.286
Start with clear task boundaries and threat modeling.

3
00:00:06.286 --> 00:00:15.196
Define what the agent is allowed to do, what data it can use, what tools it can call, what actions need approval, and what outcomes are out of scope.

4
00:00:15.196 --> 00:00:18.539
Separate trusted instructions from untrusted content.

5
00:00:18.539 --> 00:00:20.046
Limit tool permissions.

6
00:00:20.046 --> 00:00:22.590
Require approval for sensitive actions.

7
00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:27.140
Validate inputs and outputs before connecting them to downstream systems.

8
00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.398
Protect secrets.

9
00:00:28.398 --> 00:00:35.312
Avoid giving agents unnecessary access to memory, files, databases, or production systems.

10
00:00:35.312 --> 00:00:37.358
Log and monitor agent behavior.

11
00:00:37.358 --> 00:00:45.899
Test for prompt injection, tool misuse, data leakage, memory risk, unsafe autonomy, and weak approval paths.

12
00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:53.043
Update controls as the agent gains new capabilities, new tools, new data sources, or new responsibilities.

13
00:00:53.043 --> 00:00:55.368
The goal is not to make the model perfect.

14
00:00:55.368 --> 00:00:59.918
The goal is to design the system so that mistakes and manipulation are contained.

15
00:00:59.918 --> 00:01:10.135
Agent security works best when models, tools, data, permissions, approvals, validation, logging, monitoring, and response are designed together.
